<div class="pagebreak"></div>
<h2>5. Detailed reasoning</h2>
<h3>1. Assessee and activity &mdash; Business</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AB(a)</p>
<p>The assessee is an individual, resident, carrying on business. The Income-tax Act, 1961 governs FY 2025-26 (AY 2026-27).</p>
<h3>2. Cash receipt condition &mdash; 1.11%</h3>
<p class="micro">Proviso (a) to s. 44AB(a)</p>
<p>Cash of ₹1,00,000 does not exceed 5% of all amounts received (₹90,00,000).</p>
<h3>3. Cash payment condition &mdash; 1.05%</h3>
<p class="micro">Proviso (b) to s. 44AB(a)</p>
<p>Cash of ₹90,000 does not exceed 5% of all payments made (₹86,00,000).</p>
<h3>4. Turnover limb &mdash; ₹90,00,000</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AB(a)</p>
<p>Turnover of ₹90,00,000 does not exceed ₹1 crore, so neither limit is crossed. The cash test cannot change the result at this turnover.</p>
<h3>5. s. 44AE / 44BB / 44BBB income &mdash; Not applicable</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AB(c)</p>
<p>No s. 44AE, 44BB or 44BBB income is declared lower than the deemed amount.</p>
<h3>6. s. 44AD eligibility &mdash; Eligible</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AD, Explanation</p>
<p>The assessee and the business meet the s. 44AD eligibility conditions.</p>
<h3>7. s. 44AD turnover ceiling &mdash; ₹90,00,000</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AD, Explanation (b)</p>
<p>Turnover of ₹90,00,000 is within the s. 44AD ceiling.</p>
<h3>8. s. 44AD(4) five-year bar &mdash; Not barred</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AB(e) with s. 44AD(4), (5)</p>
<p>AY 2026-27 is not within a s. 44AD(4) bar.</p>
<h3>9. Low-profit limb (opting out) &mdash; ₹2,00,000</h3>
<p class="micro">s. 44AB(e) with s. 44AD(4)</p>
<p>Income was declared under s. 44AD in an earlier year. Declaring ₹2,00,000 now, below even the lowest possible deemed income, ₹5,40,000 (6% of turnover), attracts s. 44AD(4). Total income exceeds the basic exemption. Audit is required under s. 44AB(e). The assessee is also barred from s. 44AD for the next five assessment years.</p>
<h3>10. Declared under s. 44AD(1) &mdash; Not available</h3>
<p class="micro">First proviso to s. 44AB</p>
<p>Declared profit does not meet the s. 44AD deemed income, so income cannot be declared under s. 44AD(1).</p>
<h3>11. Report form &mdash; Form 3CB + Form 3CD</h3>
<p>The accounts are not audited under any other law, so the report is furnished in Form 3CB + Form 3CD.</p>
<div class="pagebreak"></div>
<h2>6. Section-wise legal references</h2>
<h3>Section 44AB(a) &mdash; Audit on turnover — business</h3>
<p class="micro">Applicability</p><p>A person carrying on business whose turnover in the previous year exceeds the limit.</p>
<p class="micro">Relevant statutory text</p>
<p class="quote">Clause (a): &quot;carrying on business shall, if his total sales, turnover or gross receipts, as the case may be, in business exceed or exceeds one crore rupees in any previous year&quot; [...] &quot;Provided that in the case of a person whose— (a) aggregate of all amounts received including amount received for sales, turnover or gross receipts during the previous year, in cash, does not exceed five per cent of the said amount; and (b) aggregate of all payments made including amount incurred for expenditure, in cash, during the previous year does not exceed five per cent of the said payment&quot; [...] &quot;the words &#39;ten crore rupees&#39; had been substituted&quot; [...] &quot;Provided further that for the purposes of this clause, the payment or receipt, as the case may be, by a cheque drawn on a bank or by a bank draft, which is not account payee, shall be deemed to be the payment or receipt, as the case may be, in cash&quot;</p>
<p class="small">Source: Income-tax Act, 1961, s. 44AB(a) and its provisos [Consolidated]. &quot;[...]&quot; marks words not reproduced.</p>
<p class="micro">Practical interpretation &mdash; simplified professional explanation</p><p>The limit is ₹1 crore. It becomes ₹10 crore only where BOTH cash received is within 5% of all amounts received AND cash paid is within 5% of all payments. A cheque or draft that is not account payee counts as cash. A turnover exactly equal to the limit does not exceed it.</p>
<p class="micro">Application to the present case</p><p>Turnover of ₹90,00,000 does not exceed ₹1 crore, so neither limit is crossed. The cash test cannot change the result at this turnover.</p>
<p class="micro">Conclusion</p><p><b>No audit on this limb.</b></p>
<h3>First proviso to section 44AB &mdash; Presumptive declarants outside s. 44AB</h3>
<p class="micro">Applicability</p><p>Any person who declares profits under s. 44AD(1) or s. 44ADA(1).</p>
<p class="micro">Relevant statutory text</p>
<p class="quote">&quot;Provided that this section shall not apply to a person, who declares profits and gains for the previous year in accordance with the provisions of sub-section (1) of section 44AD or sub-section (1) of section 44ADA:&quot;</p>
<p class="small">Source: Finance Act, 2023 (Act 8 of 2023), s. 15, substituting the first proviso to s. 44AB w.e.f. 1 April 2024 [Gazette].</p>
<p class="micro">Practical interpretation &mdash; simplified professional explanation</p><p>Since AY 2024-25, a person declaring income under s. 44AD(1) is outside s. 44AB altogether — even if turnover exceeds ₹1 crore — provided the business is within the s. 44AD ceiling (₹2 crore, or ₹3 crore where cash receipts are within 5% of turnover) and the deemed income is declared.</p>
<p class="micro">Application to the present case</p><p>Declared profit does not meet the s. 44AD deemed income, so income cannot be declared under s. 44AD(1).</p>
<p class="micro">Conclusion</p><p><b>Does not apply on these facts.</b></p>
<h3>Section 44AD &mdash; Presumptive taxation — eligible business</h3>
<p class="micro">Applicability</p><p>A resident individual, HUF or partnership firm (not an LLP) carrying on an eligible business — not commission or brokerage, not agency, not a s. 44AE goods-carriage business — and not claiming the deductions listed in the Explanation.</p>
<p class="micro">Relevant statutory text</p>
<p class="quote">&quot;Provided that where the amount or aggregate of the amounts received during the previous year, in cash, does not exceed five per cent. of the total turnover or gross receipts of such previous year, this sub-clause shall have effect as if for the words &quot;two crore rupees&quot;, the words &quot;three crore rupees&quot; had been substituted: Provided further that for the purposes of the first proviso, the receipt of amount or aggregate of amounts by a cheque drawn on a bank or by a bank draft, which is not account payee, shall be deemed to be the receipt in cash.&quot;</p>
<p class="small">Source: Finance Act, 2023, s. 16, inserting provisos in s. 44AD, Explanation, clause (b), w.e.f. 1 April 2024 [Gazette].</p>
<p class="micro">Practical interpretation &mdash; simplified professional explanation</p><p>Deemed income is 6% of turnover received by account-payee cheque, draft or electronic mode during the year or by the return due date, and 8% of the rest. The ceiling is ₹2 crore, raised to ₹3 crore where cash received does not exceed 5% of turnover. Note the denominator here is TURNOVER, whereas the s. 44AB(a) test uses all amounts received.</p>
<p class="micro">Application to the present case</p><p>Turnover of ₹90,00,000 is within the s. 44AD ceiling.</p>
<p class="micro">Conclusion</p><p><b>Condition met.</b></p>
<h3>Section 44AB(e) with section 44AD(4) &mdash; Audit on leaving s. 44AD</h3>
<p class="micro">Applicability</p><p>A person to whom s. 44AD(4) applies, where total income exceeds the basic exemption limit.</p>
<p class="micro">Relevant statutory text</p>
<p class="quote">Clause (e): &quot;carrying on the business shall, if the provisions of sub-section (4) of section 44AD are applicable in his case and his income exceeds the maximum amount which is not chargeable to income-tax&quot; [...]</p>
<p class="small">Source: Income-tax Act, 1961, s. 44AB(e) [Consolidated]. &quot;[...]&quot; marks words not reproduced.</p>
<p class="micro">Practical interpretation &mdash; simplified professional explanation</p><p>Section 44AD(4) applies where the scheme was used in a year and, in any of the next five years, profit is declared below the deemed rate. The assessee is then barred for five assessment years. Audit is required in the year of opting out and — on the literal and conservative reading — in each barred year, wherever total income exceeds the basic exemption, whatever the turnover. The verbatim text of s. 44AD(4) and (5) is not reproduced here; read it from the bare Act.</p>
<p class="micro">Application to the present case</p><p>Income was declared under s. 44AD in an earlier year. Declaring ₹2,00,000 now, below even the lowest possible deemed income, ₹5,40,000 (6% of turnover), attracts s. 44AD(4). Total income exceeds the basic exemption. Audit is required under s. 44AB(e). The assessee is also barred from s. 44AD for the next five assessment years.</p>
<p class="micro">Conclusion</p><p><b>Audit required on this limb.</b></p>
<h3>Section 44AA &mdash; Maintenance of books of account</h3>
<p class="micro">Applicability</p><p>Requires specified persons to keep and maintain books of account.</p>
<p class="micro">Relevant statutory text</p>
<p class="missing"><b>Not reproduced.</b> The verbatim text of Section 44AA was not retrieved for this tool. Read it from the bare Act.</p>
<p class="micro">Practical interpretation &mdash; simplified professional explanation</p><p>A separate obligation from audit, with different thresholds. Books may be required where audit is not.</p>
<p class="micro">Application to the present case</p><p>Not evaluated by this tool. Determine the s. 44AA position separately.</p>
<p class="micro">Conclusion</p><p><b>Not evaluated — determine separately.</b></p>
<div class="pagebreak"></div>
<h2>7. Applicability analysis</h2>
<p>Audit is required on the following ground:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>s. 44AB(e) with s. 44AD(4)</b> &mdash; Income was declared under s. 44AD in an earlier year. Declaring ₹2,00,000 now, below even the lowest possible deemed income, ₹5,40,000 (6% of turnover), attracts s. 44AD(4). Total income exceeds the basic exemption.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Consequences</h3><ul>
<li>The accounts are required to be audited by a Chartered Accountant in practice.</li>
<li>The report is to be furnished in Form 3CB + Form 3CD by 30 September 2026.</li>
<li>The return of income is due by 31 October 2026.</li>
<li>Under s. 44AD(4) the assessee is barred from s. 44AD for the next five assessment years; in each of them audit applies where total income exceeds the basic exemption.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Compliance particulars</h3>
<table><tr><th style="width:36%">Applicable form</th><td>Form 3CB + Form 3CD</td></tr><tr><th style="width:36%">Form determination</th><td>The accounts are not audited under any other law, so the report is furnished in Form 3CB + Form 3CD.</td></tr><tr><th style="width:36%">Tax audit report due</th><td>30 September 2026</td></tr><tr><th style="width:36%">Return of income due</th><td>31 October 2026</td></tr></table>
<h2>8. Professional notes</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th style="width:16%">Type</th><th style="width:30%">Note</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>INFO</td><td><b>Section 44AA</b></td><td>The obligation to maintain books of account under s. 44AA is separate from audit and is not evaluated here.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>9. Conclusion</h2>
<div class="statusbox applicable"><p class="statusword" style="font-size:13pt;">Tax audit is applicable</p><p>This conclusion is drawn on the figures and answers entered. It is subject to the accuracy of those figures and to any fact not captured here.</p></div>
